LASO-Σ — Theoretical Discovery Horizon Egidijus Kasiulevičius · Aušra Kasiulevičienė · Ąžuolas Kasiulevičius · Saulė Kasiulevičiūtė Research framework: Homo Cosmicus DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.23093955 Summary LASO-Σ proposes a new theoretical research direction for artificial intelligence centered on a fundamental distinction that is often implicit but rarely formalized as an independent control problem: Memory Formation≠Memory Persistence≠Memory Utility≠Memory Authority oxed{ 	ext{Memory Formation} 
eq 	ext{Memory Persistence} 
eq 	ext{Memory Utility} 
eq 	ext{Memory Authority} } The central research hypothesis is that an intelligent system should not determine the influence of information solely from the fact that the information has been stored. Instead, information should continuously earn, lose, or regain predictive authority according to measurable evidence. This leads to the proposed LASO-Σ principle: Remember→Measure→Authorize→Predict→Evaluate→Repair oxed{ 	ext{Remember} ightarrow 	ext{Measure} ightarrow 	ext{Authorize} ightarrow 	ext{Predict} ightarrow 	ext{Evaluate} ightarrow 	ext{Repair} } From this principle, LASO-Σ develops a theoretical vocabulary consisting of axioms, empirical law candidates, theorem candidates, formal definitions, state-space equations, and falsification conditions. 1. Foundational Axioms Axiom I — Memory–Authority Separation Memory Formation≠Memory Authority oxed{ 	ext{Memory Formation} 
eq 	ext{Memory Authority} } The creation or preservation of a memory does not determine how strongly that memory should influence future prediction. This separates storage from influence. Axiom II — Conditional Memory Authority Ai(t)=F(IH,Ri,Ut,Bi,Ei,…) oxed{ A_i(t)= F(I_H,R_i,U_t,B_i,E_i,\ldots) } Memory authority is proposed as a dynamic function of predictive information, reliability, uncertainty, binding, prediction error, and other governance variables. Axiom III — Predictive Relevance Information should receive predictive authority according to demonstrated relevance rather than merely: age; persistence; retrieval frequency; storage duration; repetition. Thus: Persistence≠Predictive Value oxed{ 	ext{Persistence}
eq	ext{Predictive Value} } Axiom IV — Null-Information Principle When historical information contains no additional predictive information: IH→0⇒AH→0 oxed{ I_Hightarrow0 \Rightarrow A_Hightarrow0 } for an ideal selective-memory mechanism. Axiom V — Prediction-Error Feedback When memory-attributable prediction error increases: Ei↑⇒Ai↓ oxed{ E_i\uparrow \Rightarrow A_i\downarrow } The system therefore does not merely learn information. It also learns how much authority that information should possess. Axiom VI — Binding–Authority Separation Bi≠Ai oxed{ B_i
eq A_i } Strongly encoded information does not necessarily deserve strong predictive authority. Axiom VII — Persistence–Utility Separation Pi≠Utility(Mi) oxed{ P_i
eq Utility(M_i) } A memory may persist while becoming obsolete. 2. Proposed Empirical Laws The following are proposed as empirical law candidates requiring continued experimental validation. Law I — Predictive Authority Law Under controlled conditions: ∂AH∂IH>0 oxed{  rac{\partial A_H}{\partial I_H}>0 } As predictive information increases, memory authority should increase, provided other relevant variables are controlled. Law II — Information Nullification Law IH→0⇒AH→0 oxed{ I_Hightarrow0 \Rightarrow A_Hightarrow0 } A memory system should not maintain high predictive authority for information that contributes no additional predictive information. Law III — Prediction-Error Authority Decay Law ∂Ai∂Ei<0 oxed{  rac{\partial A_i}{\partial E_i}<0 } when the prediction error can be attributed to the memory's influence. Law IV — Temporal Ordering Law If temporal ordering contains predictive information: IHREAL>IHPERMUTED oxed{ I_H^{REAL}>I_H^{PERMUTED} } and controlled experiments should reveal corresponding predictive differences. Law V — Persistence–Inertia Law Pi↑⇏Accuracy↑ oxed{ P_i\uparrow 
ot\Rightarrow Accuracy\uparrow } In changing environments, increased persistence may preserve obsolete information and generate inertia. Law VI — Authority–Binding Decoupling Law Bi↑⇏Ai↑ oxed{ B_i\uparrow 
ot\Rightarrow A_i\uparrow } Encoding strength and current predictive authority are independent dimensions. 3. Proposed Theorem Candidates LASO-Σ formulates several theorem candidates that can become subjects of future mathematical proofs and experimental falsification. Theorem Candidate I — Conditional Memory Authority The predictive influence of memory should be conditioned on information value, reliability, uncertainty, and current evidence rather than storage persistence alone. Ai∗=F(IH,Ri,Ut,Bi,Ei,…) oxed{ A_i^* = F(I_H,R_i,U_t,B_i,E_i,\ldots) } Theorem Candidate II — Authority–Error Suppression If a memory produces systematic attributable prediction error, reducing its authority should reduce its future harmful influence under appropriate controller conditions. Theorem Candidate III — Predictive History Ordering If temporal ordering contains predictive information beyond the current observation, correctly ordered history should outperform an appropriate permutation control. Theorem Candidate IV — Selective Memory An optimal adaptive memory system should exploit historical information when: IH>0I_H>0 and suppress unnecessary historical influence when: IH≈0.I_H\approx0. Theorem Candidate V — Dynamic Memory Authority A memory can remain unchanged while its authority changes: Mi(t+1)=Mi(t)⇏Ai(t+1)=Ai(t) oxed{ M_i(t+1)=M_i(t) 
ot\Rightarrow A_i(t+1)=A_i(t) } This separates the state of information from the state of influence. Theorem Candidate VI — Persistence–Authority Separation Persistence≠Authority oxed{ Persistence 
eq Authority } Therefore persistent storage cannot by itself be treated as evidence that information should continue to influence prediction. 4. New Mathematical Object: Memory Authority LASO-Σ introduces memory authority as an explicit state variable: Ai(t)∈[0,1] oxed{ A_i(t)\in[0,1] } with effective influence: Mieff(t)=Ai(t)Mi(t) oxed{ M_i^{eff}(t)=A_i(t)M_i(t) } This transforms memory from a binary concept— stored / deleted	ext{stored / deleted} into a continuous state: stored→evaluated→authorized→attenuated→suppressed→repaired→restored.	ext{stored} ightarrow 	ext{evaluated} ightarrow 	ext{authorized} ightarrow 	ext{attenuated} ightarrow 	ext{suppressed} ightarrow 	ext{repaired} ightarrow 	ext{restored}. This provides a mathematical basis for studying memory influence independently from memory existence. 5. New Information-Theoretic Object LASO-Σ defines the predictive historical information quantity: IH=I(Yt+1;Mt∣Ot) oxed{ I_H= I(Y_{t+1};M_t\mid O_t) } This provides a formal mechanism for asking: Does history actually tell us something about the future beyond what the present observation already tells us? The resulting research principle is: Historical Information→Measured Predictive Information→Memory Authority oxed{ 	ext{Historical Information} ightarrow 	ext{Measured Predictive Information} ightarrow 	ext{Memory Authority} } rather than: Historical Information→Automatic Influence.	ext{Historical Information} ightarrow 	ext{Automatic Influence}. 6. New Conceptual Law: Information Should Earn Authority The combined theoretical structure produces the broader principle: Information does not receive authority merely because it exists. oxed{ 	ext{Information does not receive authority merely because it exists.} } Authority becomes conditional. Therefore: high predictive information→A↑	ext{high predictive information} ightarrow A\uparrow weak information→A↓	ext{weak information} ightarrow A\downarrow null information→A≈0	ext{null information} ightarrow A\approx0 misleading information→A↓.	ext{misleading information} ightarrow A\downarrow. This is the proposed conceptual core of LASO-Σ. 7. New Research Direction: Memory Governance The framework extends the concept of machine memory into: Memory Governance oxed{ 	ext{Memory Governance} } where a system continuously determines: what is stored; what persists; what is predictive; what receives authority; what loses authority; what requires repair; what should be isolated; what presents behavioral or security risk. Thus: Memory→Governed Information State oxed{ 	ext{Memory} ightarrow 	ext{Governed Information State} } becomes the proposed research direction. 8. New Research Direction: Information Governance LASO-Σ further separates: Origin≠Truth oxed{ Origin
eq Truth } and: Behavior→Risk oxed{ Behaviorightarrow Risk } Information provenance, behavior, contradiction, amplification, and security characteristics can therefore become measurable variables in memory governance without assuming that any single source characteristic automatically determines truth. 9. Experimental Horizon The theoretical structure is supported by a growing corpus of reconstructed experiments. Particularly relevant observations include: adaptive memory improving recovery under selected regime changes; persistence producing measurable adaptation/stability trade-offs; conditional repair outperforming some more frequently repairing mechanisms; historical information producing almost no benefit in random environments; historical information producing increasingly large benefits in structured environments; correctly ordered history substantially outperforming permuted history in periodic conditions; memory authority decreasing for misleading information despite strong binding; persistence becoming harmful under repeated environmental switching. These results are evidence supporting the research hypotheses, not mathem

